Installed nine original lessons after `@computing-abstraction-review`: `computing-trust-task`, `computing-trust-state`, `computing-trust-accessible`, `computing-trust-authority`, `computing-trust-provenance`, `computing-trust-privacy`, `computing-trust-evaluation`, `computing-trust-study`, `computing-trust-review`. Computing now has **90 common learning Records**. Existing contracts, controlled-service, request/retry, communication, reporting and inquiry-assessment content are reused by exact references. `communication_study.py` remains unchanged. No existing shared learning Record gained another core edge.

The block begins with a person's task and separates proposed operations, input acknowledgement, server effect and client confirmation. Two different server histories have the same client-visible unknown state; a proof from indistinguishable observations shows why the interface cannot truthfully decide between them. Same-identity retries, changed arguments, cancellation and compensation receive distinct contracts. An accessible draft/review/recovery design and participant-evaluation plan are specified in prose. The finite program implements only the fixed reviewed request and abstract named action adapters, not the richer UI or an actual user study.

The original wrapper checks current (actor, object, operation) grants and calls the existing receiver. A matching latest receipt requires read; a new addition requires read and add. Atomic permission observation/effect, authentic identities/replies and internal durable state are assumptions, not implemented infrastructure. Revocation after a response is emitted cannot recall it. The two-object dispatcher keeps separate one-client namespaces; no concurrent-client protocol is claimed. The 68-state interaction model fixes (alpha,1,2) and permits message duplication/drop, permission changes, timeout and recovery. It proves bounded effect/evidence properties, including unknown states on both sides of commit and read-only recovery of a previously committed result.

The privacy lesson connects purpose, field exposure and retention to retry semantics. Its projection is invariant under changes to omitted name/note fields, while retained receipt fields remain linkable. Resetting the server tuple and reopening the old namespace can accept a delayed request as new; the text treats namespace retirement or retention rules as proposed designs needing further proof. There is no anonymization, deletion-of-all-copies or legal compliance claim.

Four new source Records identify bounded inspected primary passages. Robert Miller's MIT 6.831/6.813 Spring 2011 task-analysis/visibility notes were checked in selected prose with PDF positions distinguished from slide labels; current OCW CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 terms retain component exceptions. WCAG 2.2 is pinned to the **12 December 2024 Recommendation**, with selected criteria, complete-process conformance scope and separate informative keyboard explanations; W3C document-use restrictions are explicit. Saltzer and Schroeder's author-hosted 1975 text supplies selected glossary/design-principle passages, with copyright/free-access distinguished from an adaptation licence. NIST Privacy Framework **1.0, 16 January 2020** supplies selected privacy-risk and data-action passages, named without a latest-version claim. No external source media, figures or author code entered the repository. The source reconstructions apply inspected arguments to original examples; they are not replications of published participant data.

